The two massive spikes you are seeing in April 2010 (-18.1%) and April 2011 (+37.2%) are entirely authentic historical data points, not data errors.

What Caused These Outliers?

April 2010 (The Volcanic Slump): In mid-April 2010, the Icelandic volcano Eyjafjallajökull erupted, 
spewing massive clouds of volcanic ash across European airspace. This led to an unprecedented multi-day 
shutdown of European airspace, resulting in the cancellation of thousands of flights at Amsterdam 
Airport Schiphol and tanking traffic for that month.

April 2011 (The Base-Effect Rebound): April 2011 shows a massive +37.2% YoY spike. This is a classic base 
effect: normal passenger traffic in April 2011 was being compared directly against the artificially 
depressed, ash-cloud-crippled traffic of April 2010, making the rebound look extraordinarily large in 
percentage terms.

